(version 1)

# Advisory spacing around the high-impedance precision nets.
#
# The ladder taps, summing junctions, feedback junctions and reference divider
# sit at 10k-30k. Leakage from one of those nodes to a DC rail is a constant
# offset, and on 1V/oct 1 mV is 1.2 cents -- so it is worth keeping them off the
# rails and the GND pour where the routing allows it.
#
# WARNING, not error, deliberately. On a clean board this is a second-order
# effect: FR4 surface insulation resistance runs past 1e12 ohm, which at 30k
# source impedance is nanovolts. It only bites with ionic contamination --
# unwashed no-clean flux in humid air can drop SIR to ~1e9 ohm, and even then
# the error is well under a cent. Washing the board after hand soldering does
# more for this than any spacing rule. So: obey it where it is free, ignore it
# where 4 HP says no.
#
# Scoped to rails and GND only. Leakage between two high-Z nets is not a
# systematic error the way leakage to a fixed potential is.
#
# This CANNOT be expressed as a HighZ netclass clearance. Netclass clearance is
# a floor against every object including the neighbouring pads of the part the
# net lands on, and SOIC-14 on 1.27 mm pitch leaves only a 0.67 mm gap between
# pads -- anything above ~0.2 mm would make the op-amp fanout permanently
# DRC-violating. Excluding pads keeps the escapes legal.

(rule "high_z_spacing"
	(severity warning)
	(constraint clearance (min 0.4mm))
	(condition "A.NetClass == 'HighZ' && (B.NetClass == 'Power' || B.NetName == 'GND') && A.Type != 'Pad' && B.Type != 'Pad'"))
